          "id": "sec-1-6",
          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Dynamic Models from Conservation Laws",
          "content": "# Unit 1 applied project: Dynamic Models from Conservation Laws\n\n## Decision problem\n\nYou are an analyst assigned to **jacketed mixing tank, gravity-drained vessel and a transport pipe**. A proposed controller or model change claims better performance. Your job is not to accept the claim but to define a reproducible counterfactual using the physical assumptions of this unit, quantify expected benefits, identify failure modes and explain what evidence would change the decision. Use deliberately synthesized teaching data unless real data are supplied with permission.\n\n## Required technical workflow\n\n**Stage 1 \u2014 Inventories, boundaries, and state variables.** Use `A dh/dt = q_in - C sqrt(h)` to predict a measurable effect. Estimate equilibrium first, then verify the sign of dh/dt on both sides; a digital controller must not treat an empty tank as negative storage. Explain how a constant pump flow is not a fixed tank level. an outlet restriction can turn a nominal flow disturbance into a level excursion. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n**Stage 2 \u2014 Thermal capacitance and heat balances.** Use `rho V Cp dT/dt = F rho Cp (T_in-T) + UA(T_j-T)` to predict a measurable effect. Separate capacity from conductance; doubling volume doubles the time constant without necessarily changing the final temperature. Explain how an unmodeled exotherm or changing jacket temperature invalidates the simple linear response even if a curve fit looks plausible. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n**Stage 3 \u2014 Operating points and local linearization.** Use `delta x_dot = A delta x + B delta u` to predict a measurable effect. Compare nonlinear and linear predictions at \u00b15% and \u00b150% disturbances and report where the local approximation stops being useful. Explain how a controller tuned at a nearly empty vessel can react poorly at a much higher inventory where the discharge gain differs. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n**Stage 4 \u2014 Transport delays and distributed effects.** Use `G(s)=K exp(-theta s)/(tau s+1)` to predict a measurable effect. Record travel distance and flow rate independently; fit a lag after accounting for the measured transport delay. Explain how attempting aggressive feedback when delay dominates leads to oscillation because corrective actions become visible too late. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n## Evidence package\n\nProvide a diagram with input, state, output and disturbance labels; a parameter dictionary with units and evidence status; the precise model equations; a csv or spreadsheet of numerical results; two labelled charts; and a structured comparison of nominal and perturbed cases. The input changes must be safe in the synthetic scenario and no physical equipment actuation is authorized by this assignment.\n\n## Model comparison and failure analysis\n\nCompare a simple low-order model with a second model that adds one relevant limitation from **material and energy inventories; capacitance; nonlinear behavior; operating-point linearization and delay**. Keep the initial conditions and disturbance realization consistent. Calculate peak deviation, time to regain a chosen tolerance, integrated absolute error and the total excursion of the manipulated input when a controller is present. Explain any model disagreement using equation terms, not subjective plot descriptions.\n\n## Deliverables and evaluation\n\nSubmit a 3\u20135 page technical memo, runnable offline files or documented equations, a small data table and an audit statement. The recommended change must be limited to the modeled conditions. State at least two uncertainties, one additional validation experiment, and any safety question outside the authority of a classroom model. The grading priority is transparent reasoning and reproducibility, not a claimed industrial optimum."
