          "id": "sec-1-7",
          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Dynamic Models from Conservation Laws",
          "content": "# Unit 1 assessment: Dynamic Models from Conservation Laws\n\n**Assessment type:** open-book problem set. **Total:** 16 tasks plus one integrative evaluation. Show reasoning, units, model assumptions, and at least one verification check for each numerical answer. Data are illustrative rather than plant measurements.\n\n## Part 1: Inventories, boundaries, and state variables\n\n**Question 1.** Derive or interpret `A dh/dt = q_in - C sqrt(h)`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 2.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (A=2.0 m\u00b2, q_in=0.020 m\u00b3/s, C=0.010 m^(5/2)/s imply h_s=(q_in/C)^2=4.0 m. At h=1 m the instantaneous rise is (0.020\u22120.010)/2=0.005 m/s.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 3.** Challenge the approximation: Gravity drain law is an empirical idealization; orifice area, head losses and valve coefficients determine the physical discharge. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 4.** Troubleshoot the known risk: A constant pump flow is not a fixed tank level. An outlet restriction can turn a nominal flow disturbance into a level excursion. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Part 2: Thermal capacitance and heat balances\n\n**Question 5.** Derive or interpret `rho V Cp dT/dt = F rho Cp (T_in-T) + UA(T_j-T)`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 6.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (With rho V Cp=800 kJ/K, feed heat-capacity flow=20 kW/K and UA=10 kW/K, the thermal time constant is 800/(20+10)=26.67 s. A 10 K jacket step ultimately changes T by (10/30)*10=3.33 K.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 7.** Challenge the approximation: Assume constant heat capacity, constant jacket temperature, uniform mixing and no reaction heat for this benchmark. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 8.** Troubleshoot the known risk: An unmodeled exotherm or changing jacket temperature invalidates the simple linear response even if a curve fit looks plausible. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Part 3: Operating points and local linearization\n\n**Question 9.** Derive or interpret `delta x_dot = A delta x + B delta u`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 10.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (At a steady tank level h_s=4 m with A=2 and C=0.010, the discharge slope is C/(2 sqrt(h_s))=0.0025 m\u00b2/s; the approximate level time constant is A/0.0025=800 s.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 11.** Challenge the approximation: The state and input deviations must be defined relative to the same operating point; Jacobians have units and depend on point selection. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 12.** Troubleshoot the known risk: A controller tuned at a nearly empty vessel can react poorly at a much higher inventory where the discharge gain differs. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Part 4: Transport delays and distributed effects\n\n**Question 13.** Derive or interpret `G(s)=K exp(-theta s)/(tau s+1)`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 14.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (A fluid slug takes theta=L/v=12 m/(0.60 m/s)=20 s to reach an analyzer. If the downstream sensor has an additional 15 s first-order lag, its first deviation after the step still cannot precede 20 s.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 15.** Challenge the approximation: Assume plug transport and constant line velocity; axial dispersion broadens the signal and flow variation changes residence time. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 16.** Troubleshoot the known risk: Attempting aggressive feedback when delay dominates leads to oscillation because corrective actions become visible too late. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Integrative design problem\n\nFor the case of **jacketed mixing tank, gravity-drained vessel and a transport pipe**, select two lessons from this unit and build a consistent process-and-measurement model. Include one realistic actuator bound, one sensor imperfection and one disturbance. Explain the trade-off between fast response and robust operation. Present at least one table of simulated values and one limiting-value check. Label synthetic data distinctly from measured data.\n\n## Marking rubric\n\n- 30% governing equations and physical interpretation; 20% internally consistent numerical reasoning; 20% model/assumption checks; 20% alternative hypothesis and experiment design; 10% clear figures, units, and communication.\n\n### Examiner notes\n\nAnswers are not unique. A valid derivation states a control volume, assumptions, and dimensions before substituting numbers. Parameter changes should be recomputed using the actual model rather than asserted by proportion. If the student finds that the available inputs do not identify a required quantity, a defensible bound and an appropriate additional measurement earn more credit than an invented precise number."
