          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Time-Domain and Frequency-Domain Responses",
          "content": "# Unit 2 applied project: Time-Domain and Frequency-Domain Responses\n\n## Decision problem\n\nYou are an analyst assigned to **an instrumented heat-exchanger outlet temperature step test**. A proposed controller or model change claims better performance. Your job is not to accept the claim but to define a reproducible counterfactual using the physical assumptions of this unit, quantify expected benefits, identify failure modes and explain what evidence would change the decision. Use deliberately synthesized teaching data unless real data are supplied with permission.\n\n## Required technical workflow\n\n**Stage 1 \u2014 First-order step and impulse responses.** Use `y(t)=K Delta u [1-exp(-t/tau)]` to predict a measurable effect. Calculate gain from the final difference, estimate tau from 63.2%, and reserve later test data for validation. Explain how forcing a non-exponential response into a one-lag model can hide parallel heat-transfer paths or a poorly mixed vessel. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n**Stage 2 \u2014 Second-order damping and overshoot.** Use `G(s)=omega_n\u00b2/(s\u00b2+2 zeta omega_n s+omega_n\u00b2)` to predict a measurable effect. Extract the first peak and oscillation period, then cross-check damping from multiple successive peaks. Explain how a measured ringing trace need not mean the process itself has two storage elements; controller tuning can create closed-loop oscillation. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n**Stage 3 \u2014 FOPDT identification and uncertainty.** Use `G(s)=K exp(-theta s)/(tau s+1)` to predict a measurable effect. Report units, residuals, a holdout test, and confidence bounds rather than one over-precise fitted constant. Explain how a gain sign error in process identification can turn intended negative feedback into positive feedback. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n**Stage 4 \u2014 Disturbance and sinusoidal frequency response.** Use `|G(jw)|=K/sqrt(1+(w tau)^2)` to predict a measurable effect. Use at least three forcing frequencies with a stable operating point, allowing transients to decay before estimating gain. Explain how fitting a curve at one frequency cannot identify both lag and dead time unambiguously. Use the example conditions in the lesson only as a preliminary reference case; the final project should vary at least two different operating conditions so that conclusions do not depend on one curve.\n\n## Evidence package\n\nProvide a diagram with input, state, output and disturbance labels; a parameter dictionary with units and evidence status; the precise model equations; a csv or spreadsheet of numerical results; two labelled charts; and a structured comparison of nominal and perturbed cases. The input changes must be safe in the synthetic scenario and no physical equipment actuation is authorized by this assignment.\n\n## Model comparison and failure analysis\n\nCompare a simple low-order model with a second model that adds one relevant limitation from **first-order and second-order transients; dead time estimation; disturbances and periodic forcing**. Keep the initial conditions and disturbance realization consistent. Calculate peak deviation, time to regain a chosen tolerance, integrated absolute error and the total excursion of the manipulated input when a controller is present. Explain any model disagreement using equation terms, not subjective plot descriptions.\n\n## Deliverables and evaluation\n\nSubmit a 3\u20135 page technical memo, runnable offline files or documented equations, a small data table and an audit statement. The recommended change must be limited to the modeled conditions. State at least two uncertainties, one additional validation experiment, and any safety question outside the authority of a classroom model. The grading priority is transparent reasoning and reproducibility, not a claimed industrial optimum."
