          "secNumber": "2.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Time-Domain and Frequency-Domain Responses",
          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment: Time-Domain and Frequency-Domain Responses\n\n**Assessment type:** open-book problem set. **Total:** 16 tasks plus one integrative evaluation. Show reasoning, units, model assumptions, and at least one verification check for each numerical answer. Data are illustrative rather than plant measurements.\n\n## Part 1: First-order step and impulse responses\n\n**Question 1.** Derive or interpret `y(t)=K Delta u [1-exp(-t/tau)]`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 2.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (If K=2 K/% and input changes 3%, the final temperature gain is 6 K. At tau=40 s, the step response is 6(1\u2212exp(\u22121))=3.79 K above baseline.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 3.** Challenge the approximation: A real actuator step may be ramp-limited, and sampled measurements introduce time resolution and uncertainty. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 4.** Troubleshoot the known risk: Forcing a non-exponential response into a one-lag model can hide parallel heat-transfer paths or a poorly mixed vessel. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Part 2: Second-order damping and overshoot\n\n**Question 5.** Derive or interpret `G(s)=omega_n\u00b2/(s\u00b2+2 zeta omega_n s+omega_n\u00b2)`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 6.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (For zeta=0.40, percent overshoot is exp(-pi zeta/sqrt(1\u2212zeta\u00b2))*100=25.4%. With omega_n=0.20 rad/s, the ideal first peak is t_p=pi/(omega_n sqrt(1\u2212zeta\u00b2))=17.1 s.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 7.** Challenge the approximation: The stated overshoot relation requires a linear, normalized, no-zero, underdamped second-order step response. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 8.** Troubleshoot the known risk: A measured ringing trace need not mean the process itself has two storage elements; controller tuning can create closed-loop oscillation. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Part 3: FOPDT identification and uncertainty\n\n**Question 9.** Derive or interpret `G(s)=K exp(-theta s)/(tau s+1)`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 10.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (A 4% input step yields a 6 \u00b0C steady output change: K=1.5 \u00b0C/%. A discernible first response occurs after theta=12 s, and 63.2% of the remaining change is observed at t\u224862 s, suggesting tau\u224850 s.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 11.** Challenge the approximation: The tangent method and direct nonlinear least squares may produce different estimates. Input saturation should be excluded. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 12.** Troubleshoot the known risk: A gain sign error in process identification can turn intended negative feedback into positive feedback. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Part 4: Disturbance and sinusoidal frequency response\n\n**Question 13.** Derive or interpret `|G(jw)|=K/sqrt(1+(w tau)^2)`. Define its coefficient units, the state variable, its initial condition and the operating point if linearized.\n\n**Question 14.** Using the synthetic benchmark described in the corresponding lesson (For K=3, tau=20 s and angular frequency 0.10 rad/s, amplitude ratio is 3/sqrt(5)=1.342 and phase shift is \u2212atan(2)=\u221263.4\u00b0.), repeat the arithmetic, then alter one explicitly stated input by +15% and explain the sign of the difference.\n\n**Question 15.** Challenge the approximation: Steady harmonic formulas omit startup transients and rely on linearity at the forced amplitude. Describe an experiment, instrument placement and observation that would invalidate this assumption.\n\n**Question 16.** Troubleshoot the known risk: Fitting a curve at one frequency cannot identify both lag and dead time unambiguously. Propose a controlled test and a quantitative metric by which two possible causes can be separated.\n\n## Integrative design problem\n\nFor the case of **an instrumented heat-exchanger outlet temperature step test**, select two lessons from this unit and build a consistent process-and-measurement model. Include one realistic actuator bound, one sensor imperfection and one disturbance. Explain the trade-off between fast response and robust operation. Present at least one table of simulated values and one limiting-value check. Label synthetic data distinctly from measured data.\n\n## Marking rubric\n\n- 30% governing equations and physical interpretation; 20% internally consistent numerical reasoning; 20% model/assumption checks; 20% alternative hypothesis and experiment design; 10% clear figures, units, and communication.\n\n### Examiner notes\n\nAnswers are not unique. A valid derivation states a control volume, assumptions, and dimensions before substituting numbers. Parameter changes should be recomputed using the actual model rather than asserted by proportion. If the student finds that the available inputs do not identify a required quantity, a defensible bound and an appropriate additional measurement earn more credit than an invented precise number."
