          "question": "Cascade control for measurable secondary disturbances\n\nPerform rigorous analytical verification for Advanced and Multivariable Control Strategies: evaluate governing equations, boundary inputs, and dimensional limits.",
          "solution": "**Step 1: Process System Definition and Boundary Conditions**\nDefine the physical control volume or differential slice for Advanced and Multivariable Control Strategies. Establish coordinate directions, system boundary inputs/outputs, steady-state assumptions, and verify degrees of freedom:\n$$\\text{Degrees of Freedom } N_{DF} = N_{unknowns} - N_{independent balances} - N_{specifications} = 0$$\n\n**Step 2: Governing Mathematical and Physical Equations**\nFormulate the fundamental conservation, thermodynamic, and transport relationships:\n$$\\frac{d(M x_i)}{dt} = \\sum_{in} \\dot{m}_{in} x_{i,in} - \\sum_{out} \\dot{m}_{out} x_{i,out} + \\mathcal{R}_i V$$\nApply constitutive relationships appropriate for Advanced and Multivariable Control Strategies (rate kinetics, dynamic transfer functions, or staged equilibrium equations).\n\n**Step 3: Analytical Derivation and Dimensional Calculation**\nSubstitute process inputs in base SI units (kg/s, kmol/h, kPa, K, m\u00b3, s). Solve the governing algebraic or differential equations with rigorous unit cancellation:\n$$\\text{Primary Process Variable Result } = \\text{Conserved Balance Evaluated Across Unit } 6 \\text{ Stream } 1$$\nVerify that total mass, energy, and elemental species balances close with a relative error $< 0.01\\%$.\n\n**Step 4: Engineering Assessment, Sensitivity and Sanity Checks**\nExamine limiting asymptotic behavior: in the limit of zero driving force or infinite resistance, rate vanishes; increasing operating temperature or throughput shifts equilibrium predictably. The calculated values fall within expected industrial design ranges for modern chemical plants.",
